// Phase IV weekly human-in-the-loop sample - WEEK 6, criterion: safe
//
// THE DRIFT WEEK. This file is the reason Phase IV is in the project, and it
// is worth reading before running it.
//
// Between week 5 and week 6 the catalogue took on four titles carrying
// difficult subject matter - managed retreat and families losing homes
// (rec-017), an addiction and recovery storyline (rec-019), a whistleblower
// case whose verdict is deliberately withheld (rec-020). The generator started
// reaching for that material as a hook, because it is the most striking thing
// in each record. The judge's `safe` rubric was tuned against a catalogue in
// which none of it existed.
//
// Run `python run.py monitor --week 6` and read the two blocks of output.
//
//   OVERALL      specificity 0.786, band floor 0.624   ->  IN BAND. No alert.
//   NEW ITEMS    specificity 0.250, band floor 0.681   ->  OUT OF BAND. Alert.
//
// The aggregate is healthy. It is healthy because ten of the fourteen failures
// this week came from the established catalogue, where the judge is still
// good, and ten correct verdicts comfortably outweigh three wrong ones. A
// monitor that checked only the aggregate - which is the monitor almost
// everybody builds - would have filed week 6 as a normal week and moved on.
//
// The judge is now wrong about most of what the service is newly recommending,
// and it will stay wrong, and the aggregate will keep looking fine for as long
// as new titles are a minority of traffic. By the time the aggregate moves, the
// judge has been passing unsafe explanations for a month.
//
// That gap between the two numbers is the entire argument for
// `check_new_items_separately: true`, and it is one line of config.
//
// Note also that the FIXED threshold everyone reaches for first would have been
// no help in either direction here. At 0.85 it fires on the healthy aggregate
// as well (0.786 < 0.85) and you learn nothing about where the problem is. At
// 0.70 it passes both the aggregate and, being a single global number, tells
// you nothing about the new-item collapse either. The band works because it is
// computed per slice, from the raters who actually labelled that slice.

